// QUARANTINED: not implemented, not loadable, on purpose.
// Was std/core/option.orb. See std/quarantine/README.md for why it is
// not a .orb file.
//
// MISSING LANGUAGE FEATURE: a generic tagged union. Three things stood in the
// way and each one hid the next. The first landed 2026-11-01; two remain.
//
//   1. DONE (2026-11-01): the parser reads a type parameter list on `union`,
//      with the same call `parseModelDecl`, `parseFunctionDecl` and
//      `parseTraitDecl` already made (compiler/front/parser.orb,
//      parseTypeDecl). The checker then refuses the declaration, because
//      nothing substitutes an argument, and it names the declaration's own
//      line and column -- a type declaration used to carry no position:
//         $ orbit check p1.orb      # union Option[T] { Some(T)  None }
//         error[E1006]: 'Option[T]' is a union with a type parameter, and a
//         generic union has no one type: nothing substitutes 'T' at a
//         construction, so its payload type would reach the C backend as the
//         parameter's own name.
//           --> p1.orb:1:7
//      Pinned by tests/negative/n118_generic_union_decl_is_refused.orb. The
//      parser refuses the list on the two arms that share it with `union` and
//      have no substitution (`tests/negative/n119_generic_enum_decl_is_refused.orb`)
//      and refuses a constraint on a union parameter, for n53's reason
//      (`tests/negative/n120_generic_union_constrained_param.orb`).
//
//   2. Nothing would infer the argument at construction. A generic
//      `model` does monomorphise today, and the reason is that its
//      literal names the model, so the argument comes from the
//      initialiser:
//         model Box[T] { v: T }
//         fn main() { val b = Box { v: 7 }  print(b.v) }
//         orbit check -> no errors
//         orbit run   -> 7        (the C carries Box_int, not a `T`)
//      A union variant names only the variant: `Some(7)` says nothing
//      about which `Option` it builds, and no signature can name
//      `Option` bare to say it -- E1006, "a generic model has no one
//      type: a signature written before any value cannot say which
//      one".
//      This section used to claim the opposite: that a generic model
//      "typechecks and then emits a C `T` that does not exist", filed
//      as F-0007. That was true when it was written and is not any
//      more -- the bare annotation is refused at `check`, and F-0007
//      has no row in the F-code index (docs/ERROR_CODES.md:259) to
//      point at. The generic model is not the pattern that is missing
//      here; the parser is.
//
//   3. The variant registry is flat by variant name, so two instances
//      could not coexist even with 1 and 2 solved. Measured today with
//      two concrete unions:
//         union A { X(int) }
//         union B { X(string) }
//         orbit check -> error[E1005]: Payload 1 of 'X' is declared
//                        int but the value is string.
//      findVariantOwner (compiler/front/sema.orb:781) walks
//      variantRegistry (compiler/front/sema.orb:103) by the bare name,
//      so `Some` from `Option<int>` and `Some` from `Option<string>`
//      would collide the same way. This is no longer only a generic-union
//      problem: two concrete unions sharing a variant name are refused or
//      mis-bound today, and it is written up as a limitation of its own --
//      "Two unions cannot declare the same variant name" in
//      docs/KNOWN_LIMITATIONS.md. Fixing it there is the prerequisite for
//      this one.
//
// So the honest spelling for a payload-carrying union today is a
// concrete payload type per variant, e.g.
//    union OptInt { SomeInt(int)  NoInt }
//
// This file is kept as the specification of the wanted feature, NOT
// as a working module. It is not imported by anything and it is not
// covered by tests/std/. Deleting it would lose the only written
// record that Orbit wants Option; shipping it as-is would be a file
// that claims a type the language cannot express.
//
// Note the asymmetry with `result`: that one already exists, as a
// builtin type with `ok`/`err` constructors (compiler/front/sema.orb:236,
// compiler/front/lexer.orb:199-200). std/core/result.orb was an unparseable
// shadow of a working builtin and was deleted; this one is the only
// trace of a feature that does not exist yet.
//
// LIFT THE QUARANTINE WHEN: two conditions remain.
//
//   1. Each use substitutes the argument, so `Option<int>` and `Option<string>`
//      become distinct instances and the variant lookup resolves through the
//      instance rather than the bare name. `sema.variantRegistry` is still keyed
//      by the variant's own name, and the builder's `variantTagOwner` /
//      `variantPayloadType` family answers a bare name by first match, so two
//      instances cannot both be registered today.
//   2. A payload-less variant names the instance it belongs to. The design is
//      decided and is the one the language can hold: the argument comes from a
//      position where a type is WRITTEN -- a binding's annotation, a `return` in
//      a `-> Option<int>` function, a call argument against its parameter -- and
//      a `None` with no such position is refused, never guessed. No grammar is
//      added at an expression site: `Option<int>` there lexes as a comparison,
//      and this repository already refused that trade once for generic models.
//      The consequence to respect: the checker has no expected-type channel
//      today -- `checkNode` takes no expected type -- so this rides a
//      set-and-restore field like `currentReturnType` already does, and
//      `match` patterns over an instance must resolve through the scrutinee's
//      type, not the flat scan. The instance name itself must be minted in one
//      place: `instanceNameOf`-style, beside `mangleTypeArg` in literal.orb,
//      which is the leaf both sides import and the reason four silent wrong
//      answers in docs/KNOWN_LIMITATIONS.md are recorded as one class.
//
// Then rename this back to std/core/option.orb, give it a test under
// tests/std/, and write the LANGUAGE_REFERENCE entry. Lifting also moves the
// counted claims the gates read: std 20 to 21 modules, quarantined 1 to 0,
// tests/std 23 to 24, fmt 207 to 208 -- see scripts/doc_counts.py.
//
// Condition zero is done (2026-11-01): the parser accepts the parameter list.
union Option<T> {
    Some(T),
    None
}
